// PARAM: --analysis congruence
// x is the single integer 4 here and the domain knows it: the equality
// check is decided outright. The order check on the same singleton is not.
//
// The concrete mechanism is congruence_lt (Congruence_Arithmetic.thy),
// which is the constant None -- it never inspects its arguments at all, so
// no strengthening of the forward transfer can change this answer. Unlike
// the equality gap next door, this one is a deliberate floor rather than
// an unreached case for non-singleton classes: a residue class places no
// bound on the number line. It is still a floor set lower than the
// representation forces, since two singletons are comparable exactly.
//
// --analysis interval proves the order check; the reduced product in
// --analysis int gets both from its interval component.
void main() {
  x := 2 + 2;
  __voblint_check(x == 4); // PROVED
  __voblint_check(x < 9) // UNKNOWN
}
